# Everything this service currently knows about one endpoint, as RDF rather
# than as rows: the measurements and declines of the most recent run that
# recorded anything for it, and the class sample of the most recent run that
# sampled it. This is the machine-readable representation of the endpoint
# resource (see web/app.py); web/queries/endpoint_measurements.rq and
# web/queries/endpoint_content.rq answer the same two questions as SELECT
# rows for the HTML representation.
#
# Why a CONSTRUCT and not the two SELECTs. The store already holds RDF. If
# the RDF representation were assembled in Python from SELECT bindings, then
# the two representations of this resource would be two independent
# re-derivations of the same facts, and the RDF one would be free to state
# something the graph does not: a verdict rebuilt from a row is a verdict
# some code chose to write, not one the prober published. Here every triple
# in the template also appears, verbatim, in some run graph in the store, so
# the RDF representation cannot assert anything the graph does not already
# say. The only information deliberately dropped is the graph name, because
# a CONSTRUCT yields triples; the run is still identifiable through
# prov:wasGeneratedBy and the activity's prov:generatedAtTime, which every
# branch emits, the sample's branch included.
#
# ?endpoint is the input. It is substituted with a NamedNode before
# execution (SEP-0007 substitution), so it is never interpolated into this
# text. Unlike the SELECT queries beside it, a CONSTRUCT has no projection
# to list it in, and pyoxigraph 0.5.9 substitutes it anyway: verified by
# running this shape with ?endpoint absent from the template and getting one
# endpoint's rows rather than every endpoint's.
#
# Three branches, and they do NOT share one run. The measurements and the
# declines come from the most recent run that recorded either for this
# endpoint (one sweep either measures or declines every metric it applies to
# an endpoint in the same pass, so those two always share a run), while the
# class sample comes from the most recent run that sampled this endpoint,
# which can be an older one: a sweep whose classes probe timed out or was
# declined by the cost ceiling publishes no sample at all. That is exactly
# the run selection endpoint_measurements.rq and endpoint_content.rq each
# make on their own, and it has to be, because the HTML representation is
# built from those two queries and the two representations must agree.
# Because they do not share a run, each branch emits its own activity and
# that activity's timestamp: a document that named one activity and left the
# sample hanging off nothing would invite a consumer to date the sample to
# the sweep that declined to take it.
#
# A FOURTH branch, and the one that is not scoped to this endpoint: the
# newest activity in the whole store, with the three run-level facts stage
# 1c-b4 writes. A store can now hold a run that did not finish, and the two
# things a reader has to be able to conclude are "the run whose facts these
# are stopped before writing its footer" and "a newer run stopped before it
# reached this endpoint". The second needs the newest activity in the store,
# because a run that never reached an endpoint records nothing about it and is
# invisible to every per-endpoint pattern above. The reasoning for the
# widening is written out once, in endpoint_measurements.rq's header, and the
# two queries must widen together or the HTML would draw a conclusion the RDF
# cannot.
#
# What this branch emits is the INPUTS to those two conclusions and never the
# conclusions themselves: sw:emission, sw:finalised where the run has one, and
# sw:completedEndpoint for THIS endpoint where the run has one, for the newest
# run and for the run being shown. Both conclusions rest partly on an absence,
# and a CONSTRUCT emits only presence, so a derived "unfinished" flag could be
# built for the HTML representation and not for this one, and the two would
# then disagree about exactly the run this branch exists for. Emitting the
# facts instead means a consumer draws the same conclusions from the same
# quads, which is a stronger agreement than copying a flag across. It also
# keeps this document's own rule: every triple below appears verbatim in some
# run graph in the store.
#
# The newest run's sw:completedEndpoint is emitted only where it names THIS
# endpoint, matching the SELECT beside it. A run's other several hundred
# completion markers are facts about other resources and have no place in this
# one's description.
#
# Each branch's "most recent" is the run whose activity has the greatest
# prov:generatedAtTime, expressed as "no newer run recorded this for this
# endpoint" rather than as an ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1 subquery. Both the choice
# of the typed xsd:dateTime over the run IRI's string form and the choice of
# FILTER NOT EXISTS over a subquery are argued at length in
# endpoint_content.rq's header; the reasoning is identical here and is not
# repeated.

PREFIX dqv: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dqv#>
PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>
PREFIX sw: <urn:sparqlwatch:>

CONSTRUCT {
  ?measurement a dqv:QualityMeasurement ;
               dqv:computedOn ?endpoint ;
               dqv:isMeasurementOf ?metric ;
               dqv:value ?verdict ;
               prov:wasGeneratedBy ?activity ;
               sw:level ?level ;
               sw:elapsedMs ?elapsedMs .
  ?notMeasured a sw:NotMeasured ;
               sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint ;
               sw:notMeasuredMetric ?declinedMetric ;
               sw:notMeasuredReason ?reason ;
               prov:wasGeneratedBy ?declineActivity .
  ?activity a prov:Activity ;
            prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
  ?declineActivity a prov:Activity ;
                   prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
  # Whether the run these facts came from said how it was written, and whether
  # it said it finished. Written against both activity variables because
  # exactly one of them is bound per solution, the same way ?generatedAt is
  # already shared above, and an unbound subject drops its triple.
  ?activity sw:emission ?emission ;
            sw:finalised ?finalised ;
            sw:completedEndpoint ?completedEndpoint .
  ?declineActivity sw:emission ?emission ;
                   sw:finalised ?finalised ;
                   sw:completedEndpoint ?completedEndpoint .
  # The newest activity in the store, which is routinely not the one above.
  # Its prov:generatedAtTime is what lets a consumer tell it apart from the
  # activity the measurements hang off, so it is emitted even when the two are
  # the same run and the triples collapse.
  ?newestActivity a prov:Activity ;
                  prov:generatedAtTime ?newestGeneratedAt ;
                  sw:emission ?newestEmission ;
                  sw:finalised ?newestFinalised ;
                  sw:completedEndpoint ?newestCompleted .
  # No rdf:type triple for the sample, even though every committed run
  # carries one: this branch's WHERE clause matches exactly what
  # endpoint_content.rq matches, so that a sample the HTML representation
  # shows can never be one the RDF representation omits. Requiring the type
  # here to be allowed to emit it would make the two disagree about any
  # untyped sample.
  ?sample sw:sampledFrom ?endpoint ;
          sw:sampledBy sw:metric:classes ;
          sw:sampleSize ?size ;
          sw:sampleTruncated ?truncated ;
          sw:sampledValue ?class ;
          prov:wasGeneratedBy ?sampleProvenance .
  # The sample's OWN run, which is routinely not the run the measurements
  # came from: a sweep that declined sw:metric:classes on its cost ceiling
  # publishes no sample, so the newest run that sampled this endpoint can be
  # an older one. Without this the document held a single activity, the
  # measurements' one, and a sample linked to nothing, so a consumer dating
  # the sample by the only activity in the document dated it to a sweep that
  # never took it.
  ?sampleActivity a prov:Activity ;
                  prov:generatedAtTime ?sampleGeneratedAt .
}
WHERE {
  {
    GRAPH ?run {
      ?measurement a dqv:QualityMeasurement ;
                   dqv:computedOn ?endpoint ;
                   dqv:isMeasurementOf ?metric ;
                   dqv:value ?verdict ;
                   prov:wasGeneratedBy ?activity .
      ?activity a prov:Activity ;
                prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
      # OPTIONAL because only sw:metric:service-description carries a
      # conformance level today, and an unbound variable in a CONSTRUCT
      # template simply drops its triple rather than failing the solution.
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:level ?level }
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:elapsedMs ?elapsedMs }
      # OPTIONAL because a run captured before stage 1c-b4 carries none of the
      # three, and its measurements are still this endpoint's answer.
      OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:emission ?emission }
      OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:finalised ?finalised }
      OPTIONAL {
        ?activity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint .
        BIND (?endpoint AS ?completedEndpoint)
      }
    }
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      GRAPH ?newerRun {
        { ?newerThing dqv:computedOn ?endpoint }
        UNION
        { ?newerThing sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint }
        ?newerActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?newerGeneratedAt .
      }
      FILTER (?newerGeneratedAt > ?generatedAt)
    }
  }
  UNION
  {
    GRAPH ?declineRun {
      ?notMeasured a sw:NotMeasured ;
                   sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint ;
                   sw:notMeasuredMetric ?declinedMetric ;
                   sw:notMeasuredReason ?reason ;
                   prov:wasGeneratedBy ?declineActivity .
      ?declineActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
      # The same three facts as the measurement branch, on this branch's own
      # activity: a run that declined every metric it applied to an endpoint
      # can equally have stopped before finishing.
      OPTIONAL { ?declineActivity sw:emission ?emission }
      OPTIONAL { ?declineActivity sw:finalised ?finalised }
      OPTIONAL {
        ?declineActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint .
        BIND (?endpoint AS ?completedEndpoint)
      }
    }
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      GRAPH ?newerRun {
        { ?newerThing dqv:computedOn ?endpoint }
        UNION
        { ?newerThing sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint }
        ?newerActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?newerGeneratedAt .
      }
      FILTER (?newerGeneratedAt > ?generatedAt)
    }
  }
  UNION
  {
    GRAPH ?sampleRun {
      ?sample sw:sampledFrom ?endpoint ;
              sw:sampledBy sw:metric:classes ;
              sw:sampleSize ?size ;
              sw:sampleTruncated ?truncated .
      ?sampleActivity a prov:Activity ;
                      prov:generatedAtTime ?sampleGeneratedAt .
      # OPTIONAL for the same reason as in endpoint_content.rq: a sample
      # that genuinely found no classes must still report its size and its
      # truncation flag instead of vanishing.
      OPTIONAL { ?sample sw:sampledValue ?class }
      # OPTIONAL, and bound separately from ?sampleActivity, because a
      # sample is not obliged to carry this link: every content-sample the
      # real prober writes does, and the synthetic
      # web/tests/fixtures/run-truncated.nq does not. Asserting
      # ?sample prov:wasGeneratedBy ?sampleActivity from the run's activity
      # alone would emit a triple no run graph holds, which is the one thing
      # this CONSTRUCT is built not to do; requiring the link instead would
      # drop a sample the HTML representation still shows.
      OPTIONAL { ?sample prov:wasGeneratedBy ?sampleProvenance }
    }
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      GRAPH ?newerSampleRun {
        ?newerSample sw:sampledFrom ?endpoint ;
                     sw:sampledBy sw:metric:classes .
        ?newerSampleActivity a prov:Activity ;
                             prov:generatedAtTime ?newerSampleGeneratedAt .
      }
      FILTER (?newerSampleGeneratedAt > ?sampleGeneratedAt)
    }
  }
  UNION
  {
    # The newest activity in the store. The one pattern in this query that is
    # not scoped to ?endpoint; see the header for what it buys.
    #
    # An aggregate subquery, and it is the same shape, for the same measured
    # reason, as the one in endpoint_measurements.rq: the two queries have to
    # select the same run or the two representations disagree about which run
    # is the newest. Measured on this machine, over the committed fixtures
    # plus N activity-only run graphs:
    #
    #                               402 runs    1002 runs
    #   without this branch            83 ms       507 ms
    #   with it, this form            106 ms       682 ms
    #   with it, FILTER NOT EXISTS   2183 ms     32606 ms
    #
    # The subquery mentions no endpoint and projects one variable, so the
    # objection endpoint_content.rq's header raises against subqueries here,
    # that a substituted ?endpoint does not reach inside one, does not apply
    # to it; the ?endpoint-scoped lookup stays outside.
    {
      SELECT (MAX(?anyGeneratedAt) AS ?newestGeneratedAt) WHERE {
        GRAPH ?anyRun {
          ?anyActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?anyGeneratedAt .
        }
      }
    }
    GRAPH ?newestRun {
      ?newestActivity a prov:Activity ;
                      prov:generatedAtTime ?newestGeneratedAt .
      OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:emission ?newestEmission }
      OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:finalised ?newestFinalised }
      # Scoped to ?endpoint even inside the branch that is not: this document
      # describes one endpoint, and a completion marker naming another one is
      # a fact about that other resource.
      OPTIONAL {
        ?newestActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint .
        BIND (?endpoint AS ?newestCompleted)
      }
    }
  }
}
